# Copy this file to ".env" and fill in real values.
# Never commit the real .env file.
#
# ── JUST CLONED ON A NEW MACHINE? FILL IN THESE FIVE. ─────────────────────────
# Everything else below is optional and works empty. This file is the only part
# of the project that does not come back with `git clone` — deliberately, since
# it is the only part that holds secrets. None of them are lost for good:
#
#   DATABASE_URL                     Neon console → Connection string → the
#                                    POOLED one (its host contains "-pooler").
#   GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID                 Google Cloud console → APIs & Services →
#   GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET             Credentials → your OAuth client. Issue a
#                                    new secret if the old one is not visible.
#   PUBLIC_CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME     Cloudinary dashboard. These two are not
#   PUBLIC_CLOUDINARY_UPLOAD_PRESET  secrets — they ship in the browser bundle.
#
# Then `npm ci && npm run dev`. Before going live, also set ADMIN_SECRET and
# AUTH_SECRET to fresh random strings — the app refuses to serve production
# without them, and the sections below say why.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Every variable below EXCEPT the PUBLIC_* ones is read at runtime through
# src/lib/runtime-env.ts, straight from the server process's own environment — so setting them
# on the host is what counts, and rotating a secret is a restart, not a rebuild. In development
# this file is loaded into that environment for you (astro.config.mjs); a value exported in your
# shell overrides the line here.
# PUBLIC_* is the deliberate exception: it is inlined into the browser bundle at build time, so
# changing one DOES require a rebuild — and never put a secret in a PUBLIC_ variable.
# tests/runtime-env.test.ts fails if a server variable goes back to being read directly.

# --- Admin authentication ---
# The password for the /admin area. THIS is the admin credential — there is no username.
# In production the app refuses to serve /admin without it rather than fall back to a default.
# (ADMIN_USERNAME/ADMIN_PASSWORD used to be listed here and were read by nothing at all.)
ADMIN_SECRET=replace-with-a-long-random-string

# Secret used to sign SELLER session cookies. Use a long random string.
# Also a hard failure in production: the dev default is a literal in this repo, so shipping
# with it would let anyone forge a session for any seller id.
AUTH_SECRET=replace-with-a-long-random-string

# --- Google sign-in (optional) ---
# Google Cloud console → APIs & Services → Credentials → OAuth 2.0 Client ID (Web application).
# Leave both empty and the "continue with Google" button is not rendered at all (the seller just
# uses email+password) — an always-visible button whose route answers a bare 503 was the bug this
# replaced. BOTH id and secret are required for it to appear: with only the id the seller would
# reach Google and fail on the way back.
# REDIRECT_URI must match an authorised redirect URI registered there; it defaults to
# <site>/api/auth/google/callback when unset.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI=

# --- Live, but not open yet (lib/site-mode.ts) ---
# The site goes onto its real domain WEEKS before it can take money: Merchant Center review needs a
# live host and runs 3-5 business days, Search Console and the OAuth redirect URIs need one too, and
# a domain left on a parking page in the meantime gets classified as one (GO_LIVE §1). These two
# settings are what make that window safe. Both are OFF here, which is the right state in dev.
#
# SITE_NOINDEX=1 → every page renders noindex,nofollow and robots.txt answers "Disallow: /".
# It is a JUDGEMENT nobody can derive from code — whether this catalogue should be the platform's
# first impression in Google — so it is a switch, and it takes effect on restart with no rebuild.
# Turn it OFF the day the site is ready; SEO is this project's first priority and every day of
# indexing is a day of ranking. Remember it asks politely: it hides nothing from a person with the
# URL, and removing a page Google already took can take days — which is the argument for having it
# ON from the first minute the domain resolves, not for adding it later.
SITE_NOINDEX=
#
# There is deliberately NO "checkout on/off" variable. Selling is DERIVED: a production server whose
# lib/payment.ts still ships MockPaymentProvider — which approves every charge and contacts no
# gateway — refuses to take orders at all, because otherwise a stranger gets real goods and real
# stock off the shelf for nothing. Wiring the real gateway opens the shop by itself. Nothing to
# remember, nothing to forget on the one day it matters.
#
# ALLOW_MOCK_CHECKOUT=1 overrides that, so YOU can walk the purchase flow on the live site before a
# gateway exists. Named for what it does, not for what it is for: while it is set, anybody who finds
# the URL can order for free. Set it, test, unset it.
ALLOW_MOCK_CHECKOUT=

# --- Email (Resend) ---
# Transactional email (order confirmations, etc). Free tier ~3,000/month.
# 1. Create a free account at https://resend.com and verify your sending domain.
# 2. API Keys → Create → copy the key (starts with "re_") here.
# Leave EMPTY in dev — with no key the app logs emails to the console instead of
# sending, so checkout stays fully testable without an account.
RESEND_API_KEY=
# Sender address. MUST be on a domain verified in Resend. Falls back to
# no-reply@<site-domain> if unset. Example: "Dezabin <no-reply@dezabin.co.il>"
EMAIL_FROM=

# Where a CRITICAL error mails itself (lib/critical-alert.ts). Unset = the feature is off, which is
# the normal state in dev and CI: nothing is sent and nothing fails. Only severity 'critical' ever
# reaches here — a buyer could not pay, or money/stock may now disagree — capped at one mail per
# route per 15 minutes and 10 per hour overall, because an alert channel dies of noise, not silence.
ALERT_EMAIL=

# --- Custom domains (Cloudflare for SaaS) ---
# Lets a seller point their own domain at their store. With no token/zone the app uses a safe
# stub provider (no network, no account) and the platform path /<slug> keeps working either way.
# CUSTOM_DOMAIN_TARGET is the CNAME target sellers are told to point at (the Cloudflare-for-SaaS
# fallback origin); it defaults to the platform hostname so dev always shows a copyable value.
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=
CLOUDFLARE_ZONE_ID=
CUSTOM_DOMAIN_TARGET=
# Extra hostnames that are the PLATFORM itself, not a seller's custom domain (comma-separated).
# Needed for staging/preview hostnames, which would otherwise be treated as a custom domain.
PLATFORM_HOSTS=
# Dev only: set to 1 to make the stub provider report a domain as verified straight away, so the
# whole custom-domain flow can be exercised locally without live DNS.
CUSTOM_DOMAIN_DEV_AUTOVERIFY=

# --- Advertising: feed approval monitoring (GO_LIVE §2.5 layer 1) ---
# Lets the `merchant-status` job ask Google and Meta what they actually did with our product feed.
# Both networks reject a row WITHOUT telling anyone: the product keeps looking fine on the
# storefront and no ad ever runs behind it. Nothing inside the app can see that, because nothing
# inside the app is wrong — asking them on a timer is the only way to know.
#
# Leave all four EMPTY until the accounts exist. With no credentials the job reports
# "not configured" and touches nothing — the dev, CI and pre-launch state.
#
# Google — Merchant Center account id (Merchant Center → Settings → Account ID) and a Google Cloud
# service account with the "Content API for Shopping" scope, added as a user on that Merchant
# Center account. Paste the WHOLE downloaded JSON key on one line, quoted.
# The access token is minted from this key in-process and never pasted anywhere: Google's tokens
# expire hourly, so a token in this file would be a variable somebody refreshes every hour — the
# manual step this job exists to delete, failing in the worst shape (the monitor that watches for
# silent failures starts failing silently itself).
GOOGLE_MERCHANT_ID=
GOOGLE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON=
# Meta — Commerce Manager → Catalog → the catalog id, and a system-user access token with
# catalog_management. Separate from Google on purpose: the same feed is judged by different rules,
# so a row Google approves can still be rejected here.
META_CATALOG_ID=
META_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# --- Cloudinary (product image uploads) ---
# 1. Create a free account at https://cloudinary.com
# 2. Settings → Upload → Upload presets → Add preset → Signing mode: Unsigned
# 3. Copy your Cloud name from the Cloudinary Dashboard
PUBLIC_CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME=dwlzouwok
# Note: this value is the preset NAME as it exists in the Cloudinary account. It predates the
# rename to Dezabin and must NOT be "corrected" to match the brand — uploads break if the
# string stops matching the account. Rename it in Cloudinary first if you want it changed.
PUBLIC_CLOUDINARY_UPLOAD_PRESET=ShopHub
# A SECOND unsigned preset, for the invoice a seller attaches to an order — and it is separate on
# purpose. The preset above is deliberately image-only, which is what makes a product image upload
# unable to accept anything else; widening it to admit PDFs would widen it for every product photo
# in the platform too. This one allows pdf/jpg/png and nothing else.
#   1. Settings → Upload → Upload presets → Add preset → Signing mode: Unsigned, named `invoices`
#   2. Settings → Security → "Restrict PDF and ZIP files delivery" → tick "Allow delivery of PDF
#      and ZIP files". Without step 2 the upload SUCCEEDS and the link then answers 401 — the file
#      is there and nobody can open it, which is the failure that looks like a bug in our code.
PUBLIC_CLOUDINARY_INVOICE_PRESET=invoices
# Whether an image-moderation add-on is switched on for the preset above (Settings → Add-ons →
# Amazon Rekognition AI Moderation, or WebPurify). It changes NOTHING about how an upload is judged
# — `lib/image-moderation.ts` reads the provider's verdict either way. What it buys is the one thing
# the verdict cannot say: an add-on that has STOPPED because its monthly quota ran out returns
# exactly what an add-on that was never enabled returns, so without a declared expectation the
# filter switches itself off in silence and the platform keeps believing it is protected. Set this
# to `true` the moment you enable the add-on, and an upload that comes back unjudged raises an
# alert in the admin's Alerts tab. Left `false`/unset it is silent, which is honest: nothing is
# expected, so nothing is missing. GO_LIVE §2.6.
PUBLIC_IMAGE_MODERATION_ON=false

# --- Gemini (showcase-store imagery — a TOOL key, not a runtime setting) ---
# Read by scripts/generate-showcase-images.mjs only. The server never touches it, so an empty
# value here breaks nothing at runtime; it is only needed on the machine that regenerates the
# three showcase stores' photography (GO_LIVE §6.2).
# 1. https://aistudio.google.com/apikey → "Create API key"
# 2. The image models are PAID-TIER ONLY — a free-tier key returns 429 on the first call and
#    nothing explains why. Upgrade the project at https://aistudio.google.com/billing
#    ("Set up billing" next to the project; Prepay has a $10 minimum).
# 3. Paste the key here. Whole catalog is ~306 images at 2K ≈ $25-50, one time.
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# --- Payments: PayMe (the split model, 2026-08-21) ---
# Deliberately no Stripe — Israeli card clearing runs through SHVA, which Stripe cannot reach. This
# block used to say the provider "will be SUMIT or Takbull"; both were ruled out on 2026-08-09 and
# the line sat here wrong until 2026-08-23.
#
# ⚠️ SETTING THESE TWO IS WHAT OPENS THE SHOP. `lib/site-mode.ts` derives "this deployment can take
# money" from their presence rather than from a flag, precisely so nobody has to remember a switch —
# which also means a half-configured server starts charging real cards. Set them together, and only
# when the rest of §3.1 is ready.
#
# Both come from the PayMe partner account's settings (partners@payme.io opened ours). Sandbox and
# live are DIFFERENT keys; the base URL below is what decides which one they are used against.
PAYME_CLIENT_KEY=
# Our partner/API identity at PayMe.
# ⚠️ It is NOT a merchant account and cannot receive money — charging it is refused
# `174 · אפשרות זו אינה נתמכת במשתמשים מסוג זה` (measured 2026-08-23, both as a token sale and as a
# multi-capture leg). The delivery charge therefore has nowhere to land yet: it needs a merchant
# account of OUR OWN, opened with `create-seller` like any seller's. docs/payme-sandbox-notes.md §15.
PAYME_SELLER_API_ID=
# `https://sandbox.payme.io/api/` (default) or `https://live.payme.io/api/`. Left unset it is the
# SANDBOX — the safe direction to be wrong in, and `lib/payment-payme.ts#isSandbox` derives which
# environment we are in from this rather than guessing from an id.
# ⚠️ The sandbox is shared with PayMe's other partners and has no delete. Create nothing in it
# beyond the two test merchants that already exist (docs/payme-sandbox-notes.md).
PAYME_BASE_URL=
# Send THIS DEVELOPER'S clicks to a real payment gateway. Off unless it is exactly `1`.
# In production the two keys above are the whole switch, deliberately — a flag nobody remembers on
# the one day it matters is the failure `lib/site-mode.ts` exists to prevent. In DEVELOPMENT they
# are not, because the sandbox keys live in this file on your own machine: without this gate every
# demo purchase would post a real `generate-sale` to a sandbox that is SHARED with PayMe's other
# partners and has no delete, and every seeded store would be blocked for having no merchant
# account. Named for what it does, like ALLOW_MOCK_CHECKOUT.
PAYME_DEV_LIVE=
# OUR OWN merchant account at PayMe — the one the buyer's DELIVERY charge is captured into, so we
# hold the money we owe the courier.
# ⚠️ NOT the same thing as PAYME_SELLER_API_ID above. That is the partner identity and cannot
# receive money at all; this is an ordinary merchant account, opened with `create-seller` exactly
# like a seller's. Without it a cart with delivery is refused before anything is charged.
# Sandbox (created 2026-08-23): MPL17874-990088XH-20DMI0AE-UPSUETVB
PAYME_DELIVERY_MERCHANT_ID=

# --- Database (Postgres) ---
# REQUIRED since 2026-08-02. The JSON→Postgres migration (DB_MIGRATION_PLAN.md §8) reached stage 2
# and seller accounts now live in Postgres, so there is no longer a file to fall back to — `npm
# start` refuses to boot without this, and `npm run dev` cannot sign anyone in without it either.
# First-time local setup: `docker compose up -d db` then `npm run db:migrate && npm run db:import`.
#
# Local development (docker-compose.yml in this repo, `docker compose up -d db`):
#   DATABASE_URL=postgres://storekit:storekit@localhost:5432/storekit?sslmode=disable
# Production — Neon, region eu-central-1 (DB_MIGRATION_PLAN.md §2.1). Use the POOLED connection
# string (its host contains "-pooler"): the unpooled one exhausts the connection limit under real
# traffic, which is the single most common way a working app falls over in production.
#   DATABASE_URL=postgres://USER:PASSWORD@ep-xxx-pooler.eu-central-1.aws.neon.tech/storekit?sslmode=require
DATABASE_URL=

# How many Postgres connections THIS app instance may hold at once. Default 10.
# Raise only against the provider's own limit divided by the number of instances you run.
DB_POOL_MAX=

# --- Background jobs ---
# The scheduler (src/lib/jobs/) runs the periodic jobs listed in src/lib/jobs/registry.ts: purging
# expired checkout keys and lapsed sign-in counters, pulling each store's external inventory feed,
# re-checking whether running ad campaigns still have something to advertise, re-verifying sellers'
# custom domains, and asking Google/Meta what they did with our product feed.
#
# LEAVE THIS EMPTY. The default is the build: `npm start` (the built server) runs jobs, `npm run
# dev` does not — because this file holds the real Neon credentials, so an "on wherever DATABASE_URL
# is set" default would have had the dev server pulling every seller's feed URL from a home network.
# It is safe to leave on in every production instance: which one actually runs a given job is a
# durable claim in the database (migration 0007), not configuration, so N instances still means one
# run per period.
#
# Override in either direction, both rare:
#   1  run jobs under `npm run dev` — for working on a job itself.
#   0  silence a BUILT instance that shares the database without being the server (a maintenance
#      box, a load-test replica).
SCHEDULER_ENABLED=

# --- Off-site database backup (GO_LIVE §6) ---
# Cloudflare R2, holding a daily `pg_dump` of the production database.
#
# WHY there is a second copy at all: Neon's Free plan keeps 6 hours of restore history (their plan
# page, checked 2026-08-09), and that history lives INSIDE the account it would restore — a
# suspension or a deleted project takes it along. `data/*.json` and `db:import` were both deleted on
# 2026-08-03 on purpose, so nothing else in this project holds a second copy of an order.
#
# LEAVE THESE EMPTY LOCALLY. The backup runs in GitHub Actions (.github/workflows/backup.yml), where
# these are repository secrets and where `pg_dump` is installed; filling them in here only lets you
# run `npm run backup:db` by hand, which additionally needs `brew install libpq` for the binary.
#
# Where each comes from — Cloudflare dashboard -> Storage & databases -> R2:
#   R2_ACCOUNT_ENDPOINT   https://<ACCOUNT_ID>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com, shown with the token
#   R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID      Account Details -> API Tokens -> Manage -> Create. Permission "Object Read
#   R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY  & Write", scoped to the backup bucket ALONE — so a leak buys the ability
#                         to add files to a backup bucket and nothing else. The secret is shown
#                         ONCE. (That screen also shows a "Token value"; it is not used here.
#                         S3-compatible access uses only the key pair, which Cloudflare derives
#                         from it — so storing it as well is one more place it can leak from.)
#   R2_BACKUP_BUCKET      the bucket's name, e.g. dezabin-db-backup. Created in the EU location,
#                         matching Neon's eu-central-1.
R2_ACCOUNT_ENDPOINT=
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
R2_BACKUP_BUCKET=

